## Block A — Books to Scrape

### A1. Ask Permission and Introduce Yourself

```{r}
library(tidyverse)
library(rvest)
library(polite)

robotstxt::paths_allowed(
  "https://books.toscrape.com/catalogue/"
)

session <- bow(
  "https://books.toscrape.com/",
  user_agent = "EPPS6302 student; ixl240000@utdallas.edu",
  delay = 5
)

session
```

### A2. One Page, One Tibble

```{r}
parse_books <- function(pg) {
  pods <- html_elements(pg, "article.product_pod")
  
  tibble(
    title = pods |> html_element("h3 a") |> html_attr("title"),
    price = pods |> html_element(".price_color") |> html_text2(),
    rating = pods |> html_element(".star-rating") |> html_attr("class"),
    stock = pods |> html_element(".availability") |> html_text2()
  )
}

pg1 <- nod(session, "catalogue/page-1.html") |> scrape()

books1 <- parse_books(pg1)

books1
```

### A3. Clean

```{r}
stars <- c("One", "Two", "Three", "Four", "Five")

clean_books <- function(df) {
  df |>
    mutate(
      price = parse_number(price),
      rating = match(str_remove(rating, "star-rating "), stars),
      in_stock = str_detect(stock, "In stock")
    ) |>
    select(-stock)
}

clean_books(books1)
```

### A4. Many Pages, Politely

```{r}
get_page <- function(i) {
  nod(session, paste0("catalogue/page-", i, ".html")) |>
    scrape() |>
    parse_books() |>
    mutate(page = i)
}

books_raw <- map(1:50, get_page) |>
  list_rbind()

dir.create("data", showWarnings = FALSE)

write_csv(
  books_raw,
  "data/books_raw.csv"
)

books <- clean_books(books_raw)

books
```

### Checkpoint A

```{r}
books |>
  group_by(rating) |>
  summarise(mean_price = mean(price, na.rm = TRUE))
```

The mean prices by rating are approximately 34.6 for 1-star books, 34.8 for 2-star books, 34.7 for 3-star books, 36.1 for 4-star books, and 35.4 for 5-star books.

The CSS selector in `html_elements(pg, "article.product_pod")` would likely break first if the site were redesigned because the scraper depends on the current HTML structure and class names.


## Block B — Dynamic Site: Quotes to Scrape

### B1. Diagnose

```{r}
count_quotes <- function(url) {
  read_html(url) |>
    html_elements(".quote") |>
    length()
}

count_quotes("https://quotes.toscrape.com/")
count_quotes("https://quotes.toscrape.com/js/")
```

### B2. Find and Call the API

```{r}
library(httr2)

ua <- "EPPS6302 student; ixl240000@utdallas.edu"

get_quotes <- function(page) {
  Sys.sleep(1)
  
  request("https://quotes.toscrape.com/api/quotes") |>
    req_url_query(page = page) |>
    req_user_agent(ua) |>
    req_retry(max_tries = 3) |>
    req_perform() |>
    resp_body_json()
}

j <- get_quotes(1)

names(j)
```

### B3. Rectangle the JSON

```{r}
rectangle <- function(quote_list) {
  tibble(q = quote_list) |>
    unnest_wider(q) |>
    hoist(
      author,
      author_name = "name",
      author_slug = "slug"
    ) |>
    mutate(
      tags = map_chr(
        tags,
        \(x) paste(unlist(x), collapse = "; ")
      )
    ) |>
    select(
      text,
      author_name,
      author_slug,
      tags
    )
}

rectangle(j$quotes)
```

### B4. Follow `has_next` to the End

```{r}
pages <- list()
p <- 1

repeat {
  pages[[p]] <- get_quotes(p)
  
  if (!pages[[p]]$has_next) break
  
  p <- p + 1
}

quotes <- map(pages, "quotes") |>
  list_flatten() |>
  rectangle()

write_csv(
  quotes,
  "data/quotes.csv"
)

quotes
```

### B5. Browser Route

```{r}
live <- read_html_live("https://quotes.toscrape.com/js/")

live |>
  html_elements(".quote .text") |>
  html_text2()

# live$view()  # Run interactively to inspect the browser

```

The API route was faster and made fewer demands on the server because it directly requested the structured quote data instead of loading and rendering a full browser page.

The API route would also be more likely to survive a visual page redesign, as long as the API endpoint and response structure remain unchanged.

## Block C — YouTube Data API

```{r}
library(httr2)

yt <- request("https://www.googleapis.com/youtube/v3/videos") |>
  req_url_query(
    part = "snippet,statistics",
    chart = "mostPopular",
    regionCode = "US",
    maxResults = 10,
    key = Sys.getenv("YOUTUBE_API_KEY")
  ) |>
  req_perform() |>
  resp_body_json()

tibble(v = yt$items) |>
  unnest_wider(v) |>
  hoist(
    snippet,
    title = "title",
    channel = "channelTitle"
  ) |>
  hoist(
    statistics,
    views = "viewCount"
  ) |>
  select(
    id,
    title,
    channel,
    views
  )
```

## Data Notes

### Books to Scrape
- **Source and date collected:** Books to Scrape, collected October 7, 2026.
- **Permission:** The target path was checked with `robotstxt`, and the site is intended as a scraping sandbox.
- **Method:** Data were collected with a `polite` session using HTML/CSS selectors and a five-second crawl delay.
- **Cleaning:** Prices were converted to numeric values, ratings to integers from 1 to 5, and stock status to logical values.
- **Known gaps:** The scraper depends on the current HTML structure and CSS class names, so a website redesign could break the selectors.

### Quotes to Scrape
- **Source and date collected:** Quotes to Scrape, collected October 7, 2026.
- **Permission:** The site is designed for web-scraping practice, and requests were limited to one per second.
- **Method:** Quotes were collected from the site's API and converted from JSON into a structured tibble; the JavaScript page was also tested with a live browser.
- **Cleaning:** Nested author and tag information was converted into one row per quote with text, author name, author slug, and tags.
- **Known gaps:** The process depends on the API endpoint and response structure remaining available and unchanged.

### YouTube Data API
- **Source and date collected:** YouTube Data API v3, collected October 7, 2026.
- **Permission:** Access was made through a personal API key stored securely in `.Renviron`.
- **Method:** The documented YouTube Data API was queried for the ten most popular videos in the United States.
- **Cleaning:** Nested JSON fields were converted into a table containing video ID, title, channel, and view count.
- **Known gaps:** The results represent only the ten most popular videos returned at the time of collection, and view counts can change over time.

